WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.327
When Lattice fits,
and when it doesn't: what it is for,

2
00:00:04.327 --> 00:00:07.719
what it takes the place of,
and where to reach for something else.

3
00:00:08.719 --> 00:00:10.334
Think of an online shop.

4
00:00:10.334 --> 00:00:13.631
It has to remember every basket,
and every order.

5
00:00:13.981 --> 00:00:18.324
Usually, that memory is spread across
separate products: a database to keep it,

6
00:00:18.324 --> 00:00:21.567
a cache to read it quickly,
and a queue to pass work along.

7
00:00:21.917 --> 00:00:25.968
Each one is run and repaired on its own,
and each has its own idea of what is true.

8
00:00:25.968 --> 00:00:27.921
Something has to keep them in agreement.

9
00:00:28.271 --> 00:00:32.255
Orleans.Lattice keeps that memory
inside the application instead:

10
00:00:32.255 --> 00:00:34.278
one store, with one set of rules.

11
00:00:34.628 --> 00:00:38.550
It fits when many parts of an application,
perhaps in many places,

12
00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:42.769
change what it remembers, and every place
has to reach the same answer.

13
00:00:43.119 --> 00:00:44.522
It does not fit every job.

14
00:00:44.522 --> 00:00:47.921
When two places change the same
plain value at the same moment,

15
00:00:47.921 --> 00:00:51.860
only one change is kept, unless
the value is one of the kinds that merge.

16
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:57.426
In technical terms, Orleans.Lattice
is a sorted key value store,

17
00:00:57.426 --> 00:00:59.265
embedded in your Orleans cluster.

18
00:00:59.615 --> 00:01:04.685
Keys are strings and values are byte
arrays, with no external database,

19
00:01:04.685 --> 00:01:07.470
coordinator service or queue beside it.

20
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:12.221
A system is usually assembled from a
database, a cache, a queue and a layer

21
00:01:12.221 --> 00:01:16.387
of glue, each with its own failure modes
and its own consistency story.

22
00:01:16.387 --> 00:01:18.675
Against that, it takes three positions.

23
00:01:20.275 --> 00:01:22.444
First: the store lives in the cluster.

24
00:01:22.444 --> 00:01:26.383
State is held by grains in the same
cluster as the code that uses it.

25
00:01:26.733 --> 00:01:30.841
So a read is a grain call, not a network
round trip to a separate tier,

26
00:01:30.841 --> 00:01:33.271
with its own scaling and its own failures.

27
00:01:33.621 --> 00:01:37.237
And its read cache is part of the store,
on each silo.

28
00:01:37.237 --> 00:01:42.662
By default it refreshes on every read,
so a read sees the latest committed write.

29
00:01:44.262 --> 00:01:47.039
Second: conflict resolution is algebraic.

30
00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:50.494
Merges are commutative,
associative and idempotent.

31
00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:55.900
So convergence needs no distributed lock
manager and no consensus round trip,

32
00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.921
and any cluster can
accept a write to any key.

33
00:02:00.521 --> 00:02:02.894
Third: everything else is a seam.

34
00:02:02.894 --> 00:02:07.064
Storage, identity, governance,
replication, administration

35
00:02:07.064 --> 00:02:11.809
and observability are companion packages,
behind documented seams.

36
00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:14.170
A host registers what it needs.

37
00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:16.829
A capability it leaves out costs nothing,

38
00:02:16.829 --> 00:02:19.877
and each seam can take
your own implementation.

39
00:02:21.477 --> 00:02:26.159
The core alone covers point reads
and writes, ordered scans, atomic writes

40
00:02:26.159 --> 00:02:30.840
across many keys, typed queues,
a distributed lock and a saga coordinator.

41
00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:35.573
Those compose into many kinds of system:
knowledge systems, digital twins,

42
00:02:35.573 --> 00:02:38.891
distributed control planes,
platforms with many tenants,

43
00:02:38.891 --> 00:02:40.727
and collaborative applications.

44
00:02:41.077 --> 00:02:43.534
It is a platform rather than a product.

45
00:02:43.534 --> 00:02:46.180
It is not tied to one kind of application,

46
00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:50.149
and how it is deployed is
a configuration decision, taken late.

47
00:02:52.149 --> 00:02:54.609
Now, as plainly, where it does not fit.

48
00:02:54.959 --> 00:02:56.741
It lives in an Orleans cluster.

49
00:02:56.741 --> 00:02:58.753
Without one, it has nowhere to run.

50
00:02:59.103 --> 00:03:02.256
Two concurrent writes
to a plain value are not merged:

51
00:03:02.256 --> 00:03:04.357
the write with the later clock wins.

52
00:03:04.357 --> 00:03:08.736
To keep both, use one of its values
that merge, such as a counter or a set.

53
00:03:09.086 --> 00:03:12.771
When two atomic writes touch
the same keys, the later clock wins,

54
00:03:12.771 --> 00:03:15.096
and there is no single order across them.

55
00:03:15.096 --> 00:03:18.384
And no one read can see several trees
at the same instant.

56
00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:21.181
A value has to fit
in one entry of its log,

57
00:03:21.181 --> 00:03:24.994
so it is capped by the log's provider
and by the log's batch limit.

58
00:03:24.994 --> 00:03:28.921
Store a very large value elsewhere,
and keep a reference in the tree.

59
00:03:29.271 --> 00:03:31.797
And it is durable only
once you make it so.

60
00:03:31.797 --> 00:03:35.910
The default log is held in memory,
and is lost when the silo restarts.

61
00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:39.141
Both the log
and the grain storage need a durable home.

62
00:03:41.141 --> 00:03:43.114
Next on Evaluate: The guarantees.

63
00:03:43.114 --> 00:03:46.581
The links are on this episode's page
in the documentation.

64
00:03:46.931 --> 00:03:51.166
In the README, What it is and why it
exists sets out the three positions,

65
00:03:51.166 --> 00:03:53.951
and A core plus seams,
the packages behind them.
